<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boleslaus II of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/999">999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Boleslaus the Pious, Boleslav II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boleslaus II, called the Pious, was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from 967 or 972 until 999. He founded the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 973 and in 995 massacred the rival Slavník family, completing Přemyslid control of Bohemia. Late in his reign he lost Silesia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boleslaus-ii-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Boleslaus II of Bohemia" width="100" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous fresco in Znojmo (CZ) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boles%C5%82aw_II_Pobo%C5%BCny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boleslaus II, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a>, was born about 932. He was an elder son of Duke Boleslaus I the Cruel. His siblings who survived to adulthood were Strachkvas, Doubravka, wife of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I of Poland</a>, and the abbess Mlada. His mother may have been Biagota, a mysterious figure known only from her coins, whom some historians consider the wife of Boleslaus I. He ruled as kníže, a title that may be translated as duke or prince, after his father's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Like his father, Boleslaus II at first quarrelled with the Ottonian kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 974 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> supported the rebellious Duke Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> against Emperor Otto II. When Henry was defeated in 976 and fled to Boleslaus's court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-castle">Prague Castle</a>, Otto's forces campaigned in the Bohemian lands. In 978 Boleslaus pledged allegiance to the emperor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> festivities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a>.</p><p>Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> worsened from about 980. After Otto II died in 983 Boleslaus again allied with Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, while Mieszko took the side of the young Otto III. Boleslaus occupied the Saxon Margravate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, thwarting Mieszko's son Bolesław, who had married a daughter of Margrave Ricdag, and had to retire from it in 987. From about 990 he began a long conflict with Poland around Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>. In 992 he joined an unsuccessful campaign by Otto III against the Lutici.</p><p>His reign is most notable for the foundation of the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 973, which earned him the epithet The Pious from the chronicler Cosmas of Prague. The diocese came under the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, and Otto II enforced the appointment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> monk Thietmar as its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The struggle with the rival Slavník dynasty flared up from 981, when Prince Soběslav began to forge alliances with the Polish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. After Thietmar's death in 982, Soběslav's brother Adalbert, later Saint Adalbert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> until he left in 994 for a mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a>. On 28 September 995 Boleslaus's forces and the allied Vršovci clan stormed Libice Castle and massacred the Slavník members found there. This brutal triumph ensured the unity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> under a single ruler.</p><p>Boleslaus died on 7 February 999. His first wife Adiva may have been a daughter of the English king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a>, though the evidence is weak, and his second was Emma of Mělník. His eldest son Boleslaus III succeeded him but was deposed in 1002 after conflict with his brothers Jaromír and Oldřich, who became dukes in 1003 and 1012. Struggles within the dynasty shook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> until Oldřich stabilised it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boleslaus II of Bohemia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.09,14.42&amp;year=989" aria-label="Prague on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 422 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Boleslaus II of Bohemia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5330 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5330 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.09,14.42&amp;year=989">What was here in 989 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
